---
import { getImage, Image } from 'astro:assets';
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import PhotoHero from '../components/PhotoHero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Split from '../components/Split.astro';
import QuoteSection from '../components/QuoteSection.astro';
import { img } from '../lib/images';

const heroImage = img('home/story.jpg');
const ogImage = await getImage({ src: heroImage, width: 1200, format: 'webp' });

const houseImage = img('retreats/owners_house.jpg');
const terracesImage = img('farm/pergola.jpg');
---

<PageLayout
  title="Our Story — Wild on the Farm | Boquete Cloud Forest Lodge"
  description="How a hotelier from Quito traded the hospitality business for a mountainside in Boquete, and spent eighteen years building an organic farm before ever opening its doors to guests."
  path="/story/"
  image={ogImage.src}
  pullIntro="This place wasn't designed as a retreat and grown into a farm around it. It happened the other way — the farm came first, and it took eighteen patient years before anyone else was invited in."
>
  <Fragment slot="hero">
    <PhotoHero image={heroImage} alt="A living roof grown into the hillside at Wild on the Farm" eyebrow="Our Story">
      One traveler's decision to stop moving.
    </PhotoHero>
  </Fragment>

  <Section>
    <Split>
      <div>
        <div class="section-label">Before The Farm</div>
        <h2 class="lede">A hotelier who'd had enough of cities.</h2>
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          Pascal spent years running a hotel in Quito, learning hospitality from the inside —
          what makes a stay forgettable, and what makes it the opposite. It was work he was good
          at, in a city he'd come to know well. But the longer he stayed, the more he found
          himself thinking about the mountains he'd only ever visited between guests.
        </p>
        <p>
          Eventually the choice became simple: keep building someone else's idea of a good stay,
          or go find a piece of ground quiet enough to build his own.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Finding This Mountain" heading="A slope nobody wanted, at the edge of a national park.">
    <Split>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={houseImage}
          alt="The original house on the property that would become Wild on the Farm"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          The land he found in 2008 sat on the edge of Amistad National Park, cool and quiet at
          5,900 feet, with a spring running straight out of the rock. It wasn't set up for
          guests, or for much of anything — just steep, forested ground and a house that needed
          almost as much work as the land around it.
        </p>
        <p>
          That was the point. Pascal wasn't looking for a hospitality project. He was looking for
          somewhere to plant something real and let it take as long as it needed to.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <Section label="Built Slowly, On Purpose" heading="Eighteen years before the first guest ever arrived.">
    <Split reverse>
      <div class="split-photo">
        <Image
          src={terracesImage}
          alt="A pergola on the terraced farm property at Wild on the Farm"
          widths={[400, 700, 1000]}
          sizes="(min-width: 860px) 50vw, 100vw"
          loading="lazy"
        />
      </div>
      <div>
        <p>
          What followed wasn't a renovation — it was a farm, built one terrace at a time. Solar
          panels went up before there was anyone to power them for. The macadamia trees were
          planted knowing they wouldn't bear fruit for fifteen years. Sheep and hens moved in to
          work the ground long before there was a dining room to feed from it.
        </p>
        <p>
          It wasn't until 2025 — eighteen years in — that three handcrafted cabins opened their
          doors, built into the hillside the same patient way everything else here was: with time,
          rather than around a deadline.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Split>
  </Section>

  <QuoteSection cite="— Pascal, Founder">
    "I didn't want to build a place that looked like it belonged on this mountain. I wanted to
    build one that actually did — and that meant letting the farm come first, guests second."
  </QuoteSection>

  <Fragment slot="footer">
    <div class="section-label">Continue Exploring</div>
    <h2>See what that patience built.</h2>
    <p>Eighteen years of farming, three cabins, and a kitchen that starts a few steps from the door.</p>
    <div class="contact-list">
      <a href="/farm/">The Farm →</a>
      <a href="/lodge/">The Lodge →</a>
      <a href="/food/">The Food →</a>
    </div>
  </Fragment>
</PageLayout>
